  "slug": "lifespan-elegans-2c9e",
  "summary": "Recent preprints link single transcription factors and metabolic genes to neuronal identity, aging, and reproduction in invertebrate models.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is genetic control of cell identity and organismal physiology through transcriptional regulators and metabolic genes. In the Drosophila lamina, the transcription factor dFezf is reported to establish cell-type-specific sets of ion channels and receptors that distinguish L3 luminance from L2 contrast neurons. Two preprints link transcriptional and metabolic regulation to lifespan: loss of C. elegans HLH-30/TFEB alters the basal transcriptome in stage- and age-dependent ways and reduces progeny and larval survival, while disruption of the Drosophila ecdysone oxidase gene fezzik changes transcription, L-dopa levels and sex-specific lifespan, with chinmo implicated in females. A published single-cell study in Saccoglossus kowalevskii adds an evolutionary frame, showing that neuronal cell types diverge widely across animal phyla around a shared core of neuronal genes.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-27"
}
